              "locator": "§9.2.2 printed pp. 376-379 (the monochromatic plane-wave ansatz (9.43) with complex amplitudes and the polarization vector)"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-em-vacuum-wave-equation",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Source-free vacuum Maxwell fields satisfy the wave equation",
        "reference": "core-conditional-proofs.md#K7",
        "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
        "dependency_level": 14,
        "deps": [
          "post-em-vacuum-maxwell",
          "def-em-fields",
          "def-em-sources",
          "def-em-lab-geometry",
          "def-em-si-conventions",
          "cor-the-curl-of-a-curl-of-a-c2-field",
          "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field",
          "def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function",
          "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
          "thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "post-em-vacuum-maxwell": "physical-assumption",
          "def-em-fields": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-em-sources": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "cor-the-curl-of-a-curl-of-a-c2-field": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume the smooth branch of the vacuum Maxwell postulate\n[[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] on the laboratory region $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$ of\n[[def-em-lab-geometry]], with the constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]] and\n$\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$, the divergence and curl of\n[[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]], the partial derivatives of\n[[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]] and the Laplacian $\\Delta$ of\n[[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]. Let $E,B:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be\njointly $C^2$ fields of [[def-em-fields]], let $(\\rho,J)$ be the source fields\nof [[def-em-sources]], and let $W\\subseteq\\Omega\\times I$ be open with\n$\\rho=0$ and $J=0$ on $W$ (a source-free region). Then on $W$, componentwise,\n\n$$\\Delta E=c^{-2}\\partial_t^2E,\\qquad \\Delta B=c^{-2}\\partial_t^2B;$$\n\nequivalently each of the six Cartesian components of $E$ and of $B$ satisfies\nthe scalar wave equation $c^{-2}\\partial_t^2v=\\Delta v$ of speed $c$ on $W$.\n\n(a) [Hypotheses actually used] The derivation differentiates the Faraday and\nAmpère equations in time, commutes the time derivative with\n$\\operatorname{curl}$ (Clairaut's theorem, available for jointly $C^2$ fields),\nuses the curl-curl identity of [[cor-the-curl-of-a-curl-of-a-c2-field]], and\nuses the two Gauss equations $\\operatorname{div}E=0$,\n$\\operatorname{div}B=0$ on $W$. The two evolution equations alone are not\nsufficient for the conclusion without the constraint equations.\n\n(b) [One-directional] No converse is asserted. The two vector wave equations do\nnot imply the four source-free Maxwell equations: the constraints\n$\\operatorname{div}E=\\operatorname{div}B=0$ and the two curl relations are not\nrecovered, and solutions of the six uncoupled scalar wave equations need not\nlie in a source-free smooth Maxwell branch. The correctly constrained\ninitial-value problem is a separate result on this page.\n\n(c) [Scope] Only the smooth branch is covered. No decay, boundedness, compact\nsupport, finite-energy or boundary hypothesis is imposed or needed; the\nconclusion is local on $W$, asserts no value of any field or derivative at a\npoint of singular source support, and does not cover the weak branch of\n[[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]]. The result is conditional on the adopted postulates\nand makes no empirical claim.",
        "strategy": "Use the two evolution equations of the smooth branch with $J=0$: from $\\partial_tB=-\\operatorname{curl}E$ and $\\partial_tE=c^2\\operatorname{curl}B$ (the Ampere equation with $J=0$, multiplied by $c^2$), differentiate once more in time to obtain $\\partial_t^2E=c^2\\operatorname{curl}(\\partial_tB)=-c^2\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}E$ and $\\partial_t^2B=-\\operatorname{curl}(\\partial_tE)=-c^2\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}B$; commuting $\\partial_t$ with $\\operatorname{curl}$ on the jointly $C^2$ components is Clairaut's theorem [[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]]. The curl-curl identity of [[cor-the-curl-of-a-curl-of-a-c2-field]] gives $\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}E=\\nabla\\operatorname{div}E-\\Delta E=-\\Delta E$ and likewise for $B$, because $\\operatorname{div}E=\\operatorname{div}B=0$ on $W$. This is the display. The witness for the failed converse is the time-independent field $E(x)=(x_1,0,0)$, $B=0$: its components have vanishing second time derivatives and are affine in $x_1$, so both wave equations hold, while $\\operatorname{div}E=1\\ne0$ excludes any source-free Maxwell branch. Nothing else is used.",
        "physical_scope": "Conditional statement about source-free regions of the smooth branch of the adopted vacuum Maxwell postulate, for jointly $C^2$ fields on a named inertial chart with SI constants. Excluded: the weak branch, singular sources, regions with surface sources, any converse from wave equations to Maxwell, and any decay or finite-energy hypothesis. No empirical claim.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 author-hosted revision, 236 pp.)",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
              "locator": "§4.3 pp. 82-84 (derivation of $\\partial_t^2E=c^2\\nabla^2E$ from the two curl equations with the curl-curl identity and $\\nabla\\cdot E=0$; $c=1/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\epsilon_0}$)"
            },
            {
              "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall, 1999, 596-page retained scan)",
              "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
              "locator": "§9.2.1 printed pp. 375-376 (decoupling Maxwell in source-free regions to the two vector wave equations with speed $v=1/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\epsilon_0}=c$)"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Dispersion, transversality and the two polarization conditions for plane-wave solutions",
        "reference": "core-conditional-proofs.md#K7",
        "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
        "dependency_level": 15,
        "deps": [
          "def-em-plane-wave-definition",
          "pthm-em-vacuum-wave-equation",
          "post-em-vacuum-maxwell",
          "def-em-si-conventions",
          "def-cross-product-in-r3",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal",
          "thm-cross-product-norm-is-the-two-vector-gram-determinant",
