          "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas",
          "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself",
          "thm-complex-differentiability-real-linearity-wirtinger-and-cauchy-riemann",
          "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field",
          "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
          "def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-em-plane-wave-definition": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "pthm-em-vacuum-wave-equation": "physical-result",
          "post-em-vacuum-maxwell": "physical-assumption",
          "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-cross-product-norm-is-the-two-vector-gram-determinant": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-eulers-formula": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-complex-differentiability-real-linearity-wirtinger-and-cauchy-riemann": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $k\\in\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$, $\\omega>0$ and $e,b\\in\\mathbb C^3$, and let $(E,B)$ be the plane-wave fields of [[def-em-plane-wave-definition]] on the whole-space region $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^3$, $I=\\mathbb R$, with the terminology and phase convention of that definition, the cross product and inner product of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]] and [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] extended to $\\mathbb C^3$ componentwise, the constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]] and $\\hat k=k/|k|$. Then: (a) [Amplitude equations] $(E,B)$ satisfies the four smooth-branch vacuum Maxwell equations of [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] with $\\rho\\equiv0$ and $J\\equiv0$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$ if and only if $$k\\cdot e=0,\\qquad k\\cdot b=0,\\qquad k\\times e=\\omega b,\\qquad k\\times b=-\\frac{\\omega}{c^2}e .$$ (b) [Dispersion, nonzero waves, two polarisations] The amplitude equations admit a nonzero solution $(e,b)\\ne(0,0)$ if and only if $|k|^2=\\omega^2/c^2$; off this dispersion relation the only solution is $e=b=0$. On the dispersion relation both zero and nonzero solutions occur; a particular solution is nonzero exactly when its amplitudes are not both zero, in the sense of [[def-em-plane-wave-definition]](c). If $|k|=\\omega/c$, the solutions are exactly the pairs $(e,b)$ with $e\\in\\{v\\in\\mathbb C^3: k\\cdot v=0\\}$ arbitrary and $b=k\\times e/\\omega$: a complex two-dimensional family; $e=0$ gives the zero wave, and for every nonzero solution $e\\ne0$ and $b\\ne0$. (c) [Transversality, orthogonality, magnitude relation and the wave equation] For every solution with $|k|=\\omega/c$ and every $(x,t)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$: $k\\cdot E(x,t)=k\\cdot B(x,t)=0$, $E(x,t)\\cdot B(x,t)=0$ and $B(x,t)=\\hat k\\times E(x,t)/c$; in particular $|B(x,t)|=|E(x,t)|/c$. Each component of $E$ and of $B$ is a $C^\\infty$ solution of the wave equation $\\Delta v=c^{-2}\\partial_t^2v$ of [[pthm-em-vacuum-wave-equation]] on $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$. (d) [Scope] The equivalence in (a) is between the amplitude equations and membership in the source-free smooth Maxwell branch; it says nothing about fields that solve the wave equations but not the amplitude equations (see [[pthm-em-vacuum-wave-equation]](b)). The item covers ideal whole-space plane waves only, makes no finite-energy, boundary, material or empirical claim, and is conditional on the adopted postulate.",
        "strategy": "Substitution: for the fields of [[def-em-plane-wave-definition]], $\\operatorname{div}E=\\operatorname{Re}(i\\,k\\cdot e\\,e^{i\\theta})$, $\\operatorname{curl}E=\\operatorname{Re}(i\\,k\\times e\\,e^{i\\theta})$ and $\\partial_tE=\\operatorname{Re}(-i\\omega\\,e\\,e^{i\\theta})$, with the analogous expressions for $B$; these derivative formulas are the chain rule ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]) applied to the entire complex exponential ([[thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself]]) with the real part carried along by real-linearity ([[thm-complex-differentiability-real-linearity-wirtinger-and-cauchy-riemann]], [[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]). Since $k\\ne0$ and $(x,t)$ ranges over $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$, the phase $\\theta$ attains every real value; hence a relation $\\operatorname{Re}(w\\,e^{i\\theta})=0$ valid for all $(x,t)$ forces $w=0$ in $\\mathbb C^3$, by evaluation at $\\theta=0$ and $\\theta=\\pi/2$. Applying this to the four Maxwell equations and their differences gives (a) in both directions. (b) If $e\\ne0$: $k\\times e\\ne0$ because $e$ and $k$ are linearly independent (transversality), so $b=k\\times e/\\omega\\ne0$; crossing $k\\times b=-(\\omega/c^2)e$ with $k$ gives $k\\times(k\\times b)=k(k\\cdot b)-|k|^2b$ on one side and $-(\\omega/c^2)k\\times e=-(\\omega^2/c^2)b$ on the other, hence $|k|^2=\\omega^2/c^2$ by $k\\cdot b=0$. Conversely, if $|k|=\\omega/c$ take any complex $e\\perp k$ and put $b=k\\times e/\\omega$; then $k\\cdot b=0$ and $k\\times b=k\\times(k\\times e)/\\omega=(k(k\\cdot e)-|k|^2e)/\\omega=-(\\omega/c^2)e$. The vector triple-product identity is the coordinate expansion licensed by [[lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal]] with [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]. If $e=0$ then $k\\times e=\\omega b$ forces $b=0$. (c) Transversality of $E,B$ is immediate from (a). For orthogonality write $e=a+id$, $b=A+iD$ with real $a,d,A,D$ and $A=k\\times a/\\omega$, $D=k\\times d/\\omega$; then $E\\cdot B=(a\\cdot A)\\cos^2\\theta-(a\\cdot D+d\\cdot A)\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta+(d\\cdot D)\\sin^2\\theta$ and the three coefficients vanish by orthogonality and determinant alternation ([[lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal]]). The relation $B=\\hat k\\times E/c$ and the magnitude relation follow from $b=\\hat k\\times e/c$ componentwise. For the wave-equation clause, (a) puts $(E,B)$ in the source-free smooth branch of the postulate on $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$, so [[pthm-em-vacuum-wave-equation]] applies to each component. No step uses more than the listed suppliers.",
        "physical_scope": "Conditional classification of ideal whole-space plane-wave solutions of the smooth source-free vacuum Maxwell branch: amplitude equations, dispersion relation, transversality, orthogonality and the magnitude relation $|B|=|E|/c$, in a named inertial chart with SI constants. Excluded: non-plane-wave fields, sources, boundaries, finite total energy, material media and any empirical claim.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 author-hosted revision, 236 pp.)",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
              "locator": "\\u00a74.3.2 pp. 87-88 (the constraints $ik\\cdot E_0=0$, $ik\\cdot B_0=0$, $ik\\times E_0=i\\omega B_0$, the dispersion relation and the statement that the fourth Maxwell equation gives no further constraint)"
            },
            {
              "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall, 1999, 596-page retained scan)",
              "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
              "locator": "\\u00a79.2.2 printed pp. 376-379 (transversality from the divergence equations; $B_0=(1/c)(\\hat k\\times E_0)$; the warning that empty-space Maxwell solutions obey the wave equation but not conversely)"
            }
