        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$,\n[[def-countable-choice]]), inherited from\n[[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]]. Let $t_0<T$ be real, let\n$E_0,B_0:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be $C^\\infty$ fields\n([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]) on the whole-space laboratory\nregion $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^3$, $I=\\mathbb R$ of [[def-em-lab-geometry]] with the\nconstants of [[def-em-si-conventions]], and assume the constraints\n$$\\operatorname{div}E_0=0,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}B_0=0$$ on $\\mathbb R^3$\n([[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]]). Put\n$\\dot E_0:=c^2\\operatorname{curl}B_0$ and\n$\\dot B_0:=-\\operatorname{curl}E_0$. No decay, boundedness, compactness or\nfinite-energy hypothesis is imposed on the data. Then:\n\n(a) [Existence and explicit representation] There are jointly $C^\\infty$\nfields $E,B:\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,T]\\to\\mathbb R^3$ (in the extension sense of\n[[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]]) satisfying the four smooth-branch vacuum\nMaxwell equations of [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] with $\\rho\\equiv0$,\n$J\\equiv0$ and the initial conditions $E(\\cdot,t_0)=E_0$,\n$B(\\cdot,t_0)=B_0$, $\\partial_tE(\\cdot,t_0)=\\dot E_0$,\n$\\partial_tB(\\cdot,t_0)=\\dot B_0$. Explicitly, each component $E_i$ is the\nscalar solution $u$ of [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]](a) with data\n$(a,b)=(E_{0,i},\\dot E_{0,i})$ and $h=0$, each $B_i$ that with\n$(a,b)=(B_{0,i},\\dot B_{0,i})$, in the spherical-mean representation\n$u=\\partial_sH_a+H_b$ of that theorem, the mean being that of\n[[lem-em-spherical-mean-wave-identity]].\n\n(b) [Uniqueness] Every pair of jointly $C^2$ fields on\n$\\mathbb R^3\\times[t_0,T]$ (in the extension sense) satisfying the same four\nequations with $\\rho\\equiv0$, $J\\equiv0$ and $E'(\\cdot,t_0)=E_0$,\n$B'(\\cdot,t_0)=B_0$ equals $(E,B)$; the time derivatives at $t_0$ are then\nautomatically $\\dot E_0,\\dot B_0$ by the Ampere and Faraday equations.\n\n(c) [Finite propagation and domain of dependence] If second constrained data\n$(E_0',B_0')$ agree with $(E_0,B_0)$ together with all curls on the closed ball\n$\\overline B_{c(t_*-t_0)}(x_*)$ — that is, $E_0=E_0'$, $B_0=B_0'$,\n$\\operatorname{curl}E_0=\\operatorname{curl}E_0'$ and\n$\\operatorname{curl}B_0=\\operatorname{curl}B_0'$ there — then the\ncorresponding solutions agree at $(x_*,t_*)$, for all $x_*\\in\\mathbb R^3$ and\n$t_*\\in[t_0,T]$. Consequently the value at $(x_*,t_*)$ is determined by the\nCauchy data on the closed backward cone\n$K(x_*,t_*)=\\{(y,v):t_0\\le v\\le t_*,\\ |y-x_*|\\le c(t_*-v)\\}$ of\n[[def-forward-and-backward-wave-cones-domain-of-dependence-and-influence]]. In\nthe causal geometry of the adopted affine Minkowski model this cone is the\nintersection of the slab with the model's causal past of the event $(x_*,t_*)$ in the chart $x^0=ct$; by\nthe reachability objects of\n[[def-sr-causal-reachability-in-affine-minkowski-space]] and the\nstraight-segment criterion and time monotonicity of\n[[thm-sr-future-cone-and-flat-domain-of-dependence]], events in the slab outside it cannot\nbe joined to $(x_*,t_*)$ by a future causal path, so the constructed field\nsupplies no vacuum influence outside the model's causal cones; this\nidentification of the PDE cone with the model's causal cone is the modelling\nstatement of the item, not an additional empirical claim.\n\n(d) [Compact support] If in addition $E_0$ and $B_0$ vanish off a compact set\n$K\\subset\\mathbb R^3$, choose $\\rho>0$ with $K\\subseteq\\overline B_\\rho(0)$\n([[thm-heine-borel-rn]]); then for every $t\\in[t_0,T]$ one has\n$E(x,t)=B(x,t)=0$ whenever $|x|>\\rho+c(t-t_0)$.\n\n(e) [Finite-energy branch] Define\n$u(x,t)=(\\varepsilon_0|E(x,t)|^2+\\mu_0^{-1}|B(x,t)|^2)/2$ and\n$S=\\mu_0^{-1}E\\times B$ as in [[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]], and define total\nenergy by the nonnegative ball exhaustion\n$\\mathcal E(t):=\\sup_{R>0}\\int_{B_R(0)}u(x,t)\\,dx\\in[0,\\infty]$. If\n$\\mathcal E(t_0)<\\infty$, then $\\mathcal E(t)=\\mathcal E(t_0)$ for every\n$t\\in[t_0,T]$. More locally, for every $x_0\\in\\mathbb R^3$, $r>0$ and\n$t_0\\le s\\le t\\le T$,\n$$\\int_{B_r(x_0)}u(x,t)\\,dx\\le\\int_{B_{r+c(t-s)}(x_0)}u(x,s)\\,dx.$$\nThis is a branch for the same smooth constrained data with finite initial\nenergy; no existence result for nonsmooth finite-energy data is asserted.\n\n(f) [Scope] This is the smooth whole-space vacuum branch with initially\nconstraint-satisfying data; general smooth non-decaying data may have infinite\ntotal energy, and no boundary condition, inhomogeneous source, weak-branch\ncontent or empirical claim is asserted. The result is conditional on the\nadopted postulates and on $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, the latter used through the\nscalar theorem and the divergence and substitution suppliers in clause (e).",
        "strategy": "Set $\\dot E_0=c^2\\operatorname{curl}B_0$, $\\dot B_0=-\\operatorname{curl}E_0$, and solve the six scalar Cauchy problems by [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]](a) with data $(E_{0,i},\\dot E_{0,i})$ and $(B_{0,i},\\dot B_{0,i})$; the $C^\\infty$ claim and the explicit representation through the spherical-mean family $H_\\varphi=sM_{cs}\\varphi$ are clause (a) of that theorem, whose mean is that of [[lem-em-spherical-mean-wave-identity]]. Put $R_i:=\\partial_tE_i-c^2(\\operatorname{curl}B)_i$ and $Q_i:=\\partial_tB_i+(\\operatorname{curl}E)_i$. Each is $C^\\infty$ and solves the homogeneous scalar wave equation: differentiating the component wave equations and commuting derivatives ([[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]) gives $\\partial_t^2R_i=c^2\\Delta R_i$, $\\partial_t^2Q_i=c^2\\Delta Q_i$. Initially $R=Q=0$, and $\\partial_tR_i(t_0)=c^2\\Delta E_{0,i}+c^2(\\operatorname{curl}\\operatorname{curl}E_0)_i=c^2\\nabla_i\\operatorname{div}E_0=0$, $\\partial_tQ_i(t_0)=c^2\\nabla_i\\operatorname{div}B_0=0$ by the curl-curl identity [[cor-the-curl-of-a-curl-of-a-c2-field]] and the constraints. The uniqueness clause [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]](b) applied to the zero wave problem gives $R=Q=0$, i.e. the two evolution equations. The constructed fields are smooth and satisfy the evolution equations with $\\operatorname{div}E(\\cdot,t_0)=\\operatorname{div}E_0=0$ and $\\operatorname{div}B(\\cdot,t_0)=0$, so the constraint propagation of [[pthm-em-gauss-constraint-propagation]](a) with $\\rho=J=0$ gives $\\operatorname{div}E\\equiv\\operatorname{div}B\\equiv0$; all four Maxwell equations therefore hold. Uniqueness (b): any other $C^2$ solution has componentwise the same Cauchy data and, by [[pthm-em-vacuum-wave-equation]], satisfies the homogeneous scalar wave equation, so [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]](b) identifies it with the constructed field. Clause (c) applies [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]](c) componentwise, whose hypothesis is agreement of both scalar data functions on the ball: $E_0$ and $c^2\\operatorname{curl}B_0$ for the $E$ components, $B_0$ and $-\\operatorname{curl}E_0$ for the $B$ components, exactly the stated hypothesis; the cone reformulation uses the backward-cone object and the causal reachability and future-causal-path conclusions of the two cited SR items as the model's geometric language. Clause (d) applies [[thm-em-wave-cauchy-kirchhoff]](d) to each component, whose data vanish off $K$ because $E_0,B_0$ and their curls do. For (e), the local source-free Poynting identity [[pthm-em-poynting-balance]](a) is $\\partial_tu+\\operatorname{div}S=0$. The coordinate identity $|E\\times B|^2=|E|^2|B|^2-(E\\cdot B)^2$ (by expansion of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]) gives $|E\\times B|\\le|E||B|$. This and $2pq\\le p^2+q^2$, applied to $p=\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_0}|E|$, $q=|B|/\\sqrt{\\mu_0}$, give $|S|\\le cu$. For a positive $C^1$ radius $r(v)$ put $F(v)=\\int_{B_{r(v)}(x_0)}u(x,v)\\,dx$. To justify moving-ball differentiation, set $x=x_0+r(v)y$ in the integral using [[lem-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-borel-functions-via-radon-uniqueness]]; the result is $r(v)^3\\int_{B_1}u(x_0+r(v)y,v)\\,dy$. One may replace $B_1$ by its compact closure because its boundary is Lebesgue null by [[lem-unit-sphere-is-lebesgue-null]]; translated scaled spheres are likewise null by the substitution lemma. [[lem-em-parameter-integrals]] differentiates this fixed compact integral. The radius terms are $(r'/r)\\int_{B_r}\\operatorname{div}((x-x_0)u)\\,dx$, which by [[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-c-one-euclidean-domains]] equal $r'\\int_{\\partial B_r}u\\,dS$; the time term, by the same divergence theorem and Poynting, is $-\\int_{\\partial B_r}S\\cdot n\\,dS$. Thus $F'(v)=-\\int_{\\partial B_{r(v)}}(S\\cdot n-r'(v)u)\\,dS$. Choosing $r(v)=r+c(t-v)$ gives $F'=-\\int_{\\partial B_{r(v)}}(S\\cdot n+cu)\\,dS\\le0$ by $|S|\\le cu$, and integrating gives the stated local estimate. Taking the supremum in $r$ yields $\\mathcal E(t)\\le\\mathcal E(s)$. For the reverse inequality apply the same local argument to the smooth source-free pair $\\widetilde E(x,v)=E(x,t-v)$, $\\widetilde B(x,v)=-B(x,t-v)$ for $0\\le v\\le t-s$. Its Maxwell equations follow by direct differentiation, its energy is $u(x,t-v)$ and its flux is $-S(x,t-v)$, so $\\mathcal E(s)\\le\\mathcal E(t)$. This proves equality and finite-energy preservation when initially finite. Applied to the difference of two smooth source-free Maxwell solutions (the equations subtract), the same local estimate yields an energy uniqueness proof: zero initial difference has zero energy on every later ball; its continuous nonnegative density is zero everywhere, so both difference fields vanish. This supplements the scalar uniqueness argument without restricting its arbitrary-growth conclusion. All ball closures are compact smooth domains and the integrands are smooth; $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used through the divergence/substitution suppliers as well as the scalar theorem. The arbitrary-growth branch requires no decay or additional regularity. Apply Gauss propagation on the open slab and use endpoint continuity. The causal-past assertion concerns only the slab intersection. Signed substitutions use finite positive and negative parts.",
        "physical_scope": "Conditional whole-space smooth-branch vacuum Maxwell initial-value theory in a named inertial chart with SI constants: existence, uniqueness, finite propagation speed $c$, compact-support propagation and smooth finite-energy conservation for initially constraint-satisfying $C^\\infty$ data of arbitrary growth. Excluded: nonsmooth and weak-branch data, sources, boundary conditions, finite-domain problems, nonsmooth finite-energy data, and any empirical claim. Smooth finite-energy initial data retain their total energy; arbitrary-growth data remain allowed. $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is declared and used through the scalar wave theorem and clause (e)'s divergence and substitution suppliers.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
