    ],
    "items": [
      {
        "id": "ex-em-polarization-examples",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Linear, circular and elliptical amplitude choices",
        "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X6",
        "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
        "dependency_level": 17,
        "deps": [
          "def-em-polarization-definition",
          "pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell",
          "def-em-plane-wave-definition",
          "def-cross-product-in-r3",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "def-em-si-conventions",
          "def-em-lab-geometry"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-em-polarization-definition": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell": "physical-result",
          "def-em-plane-wave-definition": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-em-lab-geometry": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Use the frame basis $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ and the SI conventions of\n[[def-em-lab-geometry]] and [[def-em-si-conventions]]. Fix $K>0$, put\n$\\omega:=cK$ and $k:=Ke_3$, so that $|k|=K=\\omega/c$ and $\\hat k=e_3$, and let\n$E_0>0$ carry the unit $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$. For each amplitude choice\nbelow put $b:=k\\times e/\\omega$; then the amplitude conditions of\n[[pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell]] hold and the corresponding fields\n$(E,B)$ of [[def-em-plane-wave-definition]] are smooth source-free vacuum\nMaxwell solutions on $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$, with\n$\\theta(x,t)=Kx_3-\\omega t$.\n\n(i) [Linear] $e:=E_0e_1$, $b=(E_0/c)e_2$. Here $a=E_0e_1$ and $d=0$, the\ntrace is the segment $[-E_0,E_0]e_1=\\{se_1:-E_0\\le s\\le E_0\\}$ traversed\ntwice per period, the polarization is linear and\n$H=\\langle a\\times d,\\hat k\\rangle=0$; moreover\n$E=E_0\\cos\\theta\\,e_1$, $B=(E_0/c)\\cos\\theta\\,e_2$ and $|B|=|E|/c$.\n\n(ii) [Circular] $e:=E_0(e_1+ie_2)$, $b=(E_0/c)(e_2-ie_1)$. Here\n$a=E_0e_1$ and $d=E_0e_2$, so $|a|=|d|=E_0$ and $\\langle a,d\\rangle=0$: the\npolarization is circular of radius $E_0$, with\n$E=E_0(\\cos\\theta\\,e_1-\\sin\\theta\\,e_2)$,\n$B=(E_0/c)(\\sin\\theta\\,e_1+\\cos\\theta\\,e_2)$, $|E|=E_0$ and $|B|=E_0/c$\neverywhere, and $H=\\langle a\\times d,\\hat k\\rangle=E_0^2>0$, so this\npolarization is left-handed in the convention of\n[[def-em-polarization-definition]]. The reversed choice $e=E_0(e_1-ie_2)$\ngives $H=-E_0^2<0$ and is right-handed.\n\n(iii) [Elliptical] $e:=E_0(e_1+2ie_2)$. Here $a=E_0e_1$ and $d=2E_0e_2$ are\northogonal with unequal lengths, so by clause (b)(iii) of\n[[def-em-polarization-definition]] the trace is the axis-aligned ellipse\n$E_0\\cos\\theta\\,e_1-2E_0\\sin\\theta\\,e_2$ with semiaxes $E_0$ and $2E_0$ along\n$e_1$ and $e_2$; $|E|^2=E_0^2(\\cos^2\\theta+4\\sin^2\\theta)$ varies between\n$E_0^2$ and $4E_0^2$; and $b=(E_0/c)(e_2-2ie_1)$, $H=2E_0^2>0$.\n\n(iv) [Degenerate and edge cases] $e=0$ gives the zero wave, for which\n$a=d=0$ and the trace is the single point $\\{0\\}$. For $e:=E_0(1+i)e_1$ one\nhas $a=d=E_0e_1$, so the polarization is linear with trace the segment\n$[-\\sqrt2E_0,\\sqrt2E_0]e_1$ and $H=0$; the classification (b) of\n[[def-em-polarization-definition]] covers this case without requiring $a$ and\n$d$ to be orthogonal.\n\n(v) [Verification and scope] In every case $b=k\\times e/\\omega$ with\n$|k|=\\omega/c$, so the amplitude equations hold and the fields are smooth\nsource-free solutions by [[pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell]]; the amplitudes and\nthe semiaxis lengths carry the unit $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and\n$|B|=|E|/c$ holds pointwise. The examples assert no empirical realization and\nno measured polarization.",
        "strategy": "Direct substitution: for each choice compute $a=\\operatorname{Re}e$, $d=\\operatorname{Im}e$, the trace $a\\cos\\theta-d\\sin\\theta$, the helicity sign $H=\\langle a\\times d,\\hat k\\rangle$ with $\\hat k=e_3$, and $b=k\\times e/\\omega$; then apply the classification clauses (b),(c) of [[def-em-polarization-definition]] and the amplitude conditions of [[pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell]](b). For (ii) the identity $|E|=E_0$ follows from $|a\\cos\\theta-d\\sin\\theta|^2=|a|^2\\cos^2\\theta+|d|^2\\sin^2\\theta$ when $\\langle a,d\\rangle=0$ and $|a|=|d|$. For (iv) the trace values are $E_0(\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta)$, whose range is $[-\\sqrt2E_0,\\sqrt2E_0]$. Nothing beyond the listed suppliers is used, and no physical interpretation is added.",
        "physical_scope": "Explicit ideal plane-wave amplitude choices on $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$ in a named inertial frame with SI units: linear, circular (both handedness signs), elliptical with orthogonal axes, the zero wave and one dependent-amplitude linear edge case. Excluded: partially polarized light, superposition with other waves, and any empirical or measurement claim.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 author-hosted revision, 236 pp.)",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
              "locator": "§4.3.2 pp. 87-88 (linear polarization for real amplitudes; circular and elliptic polarization for $E_0=\\alpha-i\\beta$ with the stated handedness convention)"
            },
            {
              "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall, 1999, 596-page retained scan)",
              "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
              "locator": "§9.1.4 and Problem 9.8 printed pp. 373-375 (the two independent linear polarization states and the circular case with the clockwise/counterclockwise naming)"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "ex-em-plane-wave-infinite-energy",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "A nonzero whole-space plane wave has infinite total energy",
        "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X6",
        "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
        "dependency_level": 20,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "pthm-em-wave-energy-average",
          "pthm-em-general-wave-ivp",
          "def-em-vacuum-energy-flux",
          "def-em-plane-wave-definition",
          "def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set",
          "def-multidimensional-rectangle-and-volume",
          "def-multidimensional-darboux-integral",
          "thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell",
          "lem-em-period-average"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "pthm-em-wave-energy-average": "physical-result",
          "pthm-em-general-wave-ivp": "physical-result",
          "def-em-vacuum-energy-flux": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-em-plane-wave-definition": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-multidimensional-rectangle-and-volume": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-multidimensional-darboux-integral": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
          "pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell": "physical-result",
          "lem-em-period-average": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, [[def-countable-choice]]) for the Lebesgue-energy and compact-source comparison branches (ii), (iii), as required by their measure and wave-Cauchy suppliers. The explicit plane-wave fields of (i) use no choice.\n\nFix $K>0$, $\\omega:=cK$, $k:=Ke_3$, $E_0>0$\n(unit $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$), the amplitude $e:=E_0e_1$ and\n$b:=k\\times e/\\omega=(E_0/c)e_2$, and let $(E,B)$ be the corresponding\nplane-wave solution of [[pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell]]\n([[def-em-plane-wave-definition]]) on $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$ with energy\ndensity $u$ of [[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]]; write\n$\\theta(x,t)=Kx_3-\\omega t$. Then:\n\n(i) [Explicit density] $E=E_0\\cos\\theta\\,e_1$,\n$B=(E_0/c)\\cos\\theta\\,e_2$ and\n$u(x,t)=\\varepsilon_0E_0^2\\cos^2\\theta(x,t)$, a nonnegative function vanishing\nexactly on the nodal planes $\\cos\\theta=0$; the period average at every fixed\n$x$ is $\\langle u\\rangle=\\varepsilon_0E_0^2/2>0$, uniformly in $x$ and $t$\n([[pthm-em-wave-energy-average]](a)).\n\n(ii) [Unbounded box integrals] Fix $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and let\n$R\\ge(\\omega|t|+\\pi/4)/K$. On the sub-box\n$Q_R:=[-R,R]^2\\times[(\\omega t-\\pi/4)/K,\\,(\\omega t+\\pi/4)/K]$ of the box\n$[-R,R]^3$ one has $\\cos^2\\theta\\ge1/2$, hence\n$u\\ge\\varepsilon_0E_0^2/2$ there; both boxes are product boxes with finite\nvolume ([[def-multidimensional-rectangle-and-volume]]), $u$ is continuous on\nthem and therefore Riemann integrable\n([[thm-continuous-functions-on-compact-jordan-sets-are-integrable]]), and\nmonotonicity of the multidimensional Darboux integral\n([[def-multidimensional-darboux-integral]],\n[[def-riemann-integral-over-a-jordan-set]]) gives\n$$\\int_{[-R,R]^3}u(x,t)\\,dx\\;\\ge\\;\\int_{Q_R}u(x,t)\\,dx\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{\\varepsilon_0E_0^2}{2}\\,\\mathrm{vol}(Q_R)\\;=\\;\\frac{\\pi\\varepsilon_0E_0^2}{K}\\,R^2\\;\\longrightarrow\\;+\\infty\\quad(R\\to\\infty).$$\nConsequently the box integrals have no finite limit as\n$R\\to\\infty$: the ideal plane wave has infinite total energy in the\nbox-exhaustion sense, in contrast to the total energy of a single wave 'mode'\nbeing finite on any bounded box.\n\n(iii) [Contrast with compactly supported data] If instead the constrained data\nof [[pthm-em-general-wave-ivp]] vanish off a compact set, then by clause (d) of\nthat theorem the fields have compact spatial support at each time, and the\ninstantaneous total energy is the integral of the continuous density $u$\nover a bounding closed box containing its support, hence finite. So finite total energy is available for\ncompactly supported data, while the ideal plane wave of (i)-(ii) is not a\nfinite-energy configuration.\n\n(iv) [Scope] The example is an exact computation for the ideal plane wave; it\nreports no measurement, and \"infinite total energy\" means the divergence\nstatement (ii), not a claim about any laboratory field or finite beam.",
        "strategy": "For (i) use $k\\times e=(Ke_3)\\times(E_0e_1)=KE_0e_2$ and the definitions of $E,B,u$: $u=(\\varepsilon_0/2)|E|^2+(1/(2\\mu_0))|B|^2=\\varepsilon_0E_0^2\\cos^2\\theta$ by $1/(\\mu_0c^2)=\\varepsilon_0$, and $\\langle\\cos^2\\rangle=1/2$ is the real-amplitude case of [[lem-em-period-average]] (equivalently (a) of [[pthm-em-wave-energy-average]] with $|e|^2=E_0^2$). For (ii) the range condition on $R$ places $Q_R$ inside $[-R,R]^3$; on $Q_R$, $|Kx_3-\\omega t|\\le\\pi/4$ gives $\\cos^2\\theta\\ge1/2$, and the comparison of integrals is the elementary monotonicity of the multidimensional Darboux integral for continuous integrands over boxes, with $\\mathrm{vol}(Q_R)=(2R)^2\\cdot\\pi/(2K)$ from [[def-multidimensional-rectangle-and-volume]]. The bound is $\\pi\\varepsilon_0E_0^2R^2/K\\to\\infty$. Part (iii) quotes clause (d) of [[pthm-em-general-wave-ivp]] and the finite integral of its continuous density over a nondegenerate bounding closed box, with zero density outside. No empirical input is used.",
        "physical_scope": "An exact computation for one explicit ideal plane-wave configuration on $\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R$: the positivity and uniformity of the period-averaged energy density, the divergence of the box integrals, and the contrast with compactly supported finite-energy data. No measured quantity, no finite beam and no empirical claim.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 author-hosted revision, 236 pp.)",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
              "locator": "§4.4 pp. 92-94 (the plane-wave energy density $u=\\epsilon_0E_0^2\\sin^2(k\\cdot x-\\omega t)$ and $\\bar u=\\epsilon_0E_0^2/2$, positive and uniform on average)"
            },
            {
              "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall, 1999, 596-page retained scan)",
              "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
              "locator": "§9.2.2 printed pp. 376-379 and §9.2.3 printed p. 381 (the monochromatic plane wave is an idealisation on all of space; the energy density is $\\frac12\\epsilon_0E_0^2$ on average and never identically zero)"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "texp-em-radiation-pressure-example",
