        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$,\n[[def-countable-choice]]). Idealised setup: the vacuum region is\n$\\{x_3<0\\}$ and a material body occupies $\\{x_3>0\\}$ with plane surface\n$x_3=0$; an incident plane wave of [[def-em-plane-wave-definition]] with\n$k=Ke_3$ ($K>0$, $\\omega=cK$), $e=E_0e_1$ ($E_0>0$, unit\n$\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$) and $b=(E_0/c)e_2$ propagates in the $+e_3$\ndirection. By [[pthm-em-wave-energy-average]] its period-averaged Poynting\nflux is $\\langle S\\rangle=I\\hat k$ with $I=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0c}{2}E_0^2$, and\nits period-averaged normal Maxwell stress is $\\langle\\sigma_{33}\\rangle=-I/c$\n([[def-em-vacuum-stress-momentum]] for $\\sigma$). All period-averaged\nquantities below are independent of time. Let $A>0$ be the area (in\n$\\mathrm m^2$) of a finite rectangular surface patch, let\n$F\\in\\mathbb R^3$ be its period-averaged force (in N), and let $p\\in\\mathbb R$\nbe the signed normal force per unit area (in $\\mathrm N\\,\\mathrm m^{-2}$).\nThe adopted boundary-force law below sets $F=A p e_3$; these are patch\nquantities in the uniform infinite-plane model. Two idealised models of the surface:\n\n(i) [Ideal absorber] Adopt (A1) no reflected vacuum field and a zero back-face\nstress for an ideal absorption layer, and (A2) the force on each surface patch\nis given by the pillbox limit of the momentum balance, i.e. its signed pressure is the difference\n$\\langle\\sigma_{33}\\rangle_{\\mathrm{back}}-\\langle\\sigma_{33}(0^-)\\rangle$ of\nthe back-face and incident-side normal stresses, with the back-face stress set\nto zero by (A1). This is an exterior boundary-force model: the incident vacuum\nfield is not asserted to extend by zero across a sharp interface as a global\nMaxwell solution. A real absorption layer must supply a compatible\nmaterial-field solution; no such realization is claimed here. The incident-wave average is constant throughout the vacuum region by\n[[pthm-em-wave-energy-average]](c), so its one-sided surface limit is\n$\\langle\\sigma_{33}(0^-)\\rangle=-I/c$. Then the force per unit area is\n$p=I/c$ and $F/A=(I/c)\\,\\hat k$: the wave pushes the absorber along its direction of\npropagation with pressure $I/c$.\n\n(ii) [Ideal reflector] Adopt instead (A1') the field on the material side\nvanishes identically while the vacuum field is the sum of the incident wave and\nthe reflected plane wave with $k'=-k$, $e'=-e$ (so that the tangential\nelectric field vanishes on $x_3=0$; the sum is a source-free solution by\n[[pthm-em-linear-superposition]]), together with the same boundary-force law\n(A2) with zero material-side stress. Compute the averaged normal stress of the\nstanding wave directly: the real fields satisfy\n$E=E_0[\\cos(Kx_3-\\omega t)-\\cos(Kx_3+\\omega t)]e_1=2E_0\\sin(Kx_3)\\sin(\\omega t)e_1$\nand $B=(2E_0/c)\\cos(Kx_3)\\cos(\\omega t)e_2$, whose cross terms cancel\nidentically in $\\sigma_{33}=-(\\varepsilon_0/2)|E|^2-(1/(2\\mu_0))|B|^2$; with\n$\\langle\\sin^2\\rangle=\\langle\\cos^2\\rangle=1/2$ this gives\n$\\langle\\sigma_{33}(0^-)\\rangle=-\\varepsilon_0E_0^2=-2I/c$ (the incident and\nreflected waves contribute $-I/c$ each). Hence $p=2I/c$ and $F/A=(2I/c)\\,\\hat k$: twice the\nabsorbing pressure, because the momentum of the reflected wave is reversed.\n\n(iii) [Status] The absorber back-face and reflector vanishing-field\nidealisations (A1)/(A1') and the boundary-force law (A2) are adopted model\nassumptions of this thought experiment, not derived material laws and not\nobservations; the deduction from them with the computed averaged stresses is\ncomplete and conditional. The setup idealises an infinite plane beam at normal\nincidence on an infinite plane surface; oblique incidence, finite beams, real\nabsorbers and any magnetisation/current response of a material require a\nmaterial model and are excluded. No measured pressure is reported and no\nempirical or statistical claim is made.",
        "strategy": "Deduction from the stated idealisations. Incident wave data: $k\\times e=KE_0e_2$, so the amplitudes satisfy [[pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell]](b) and (c) of [[pthm-em-wave-energy-average]] gives $I=\\varepsilon_0cE_0^2/2$ and $\\langle\\sigma_{33}\\rangle=-I/c$ for a single monochromatic wave. In (i), the vacuum fields are the incident wave alone, so the already computed constant algebraic average gives $\\langle\\sigma_{33}\\rangle=-I/c$ throughout the vacuum region and its one-sided limit at $0^-$ equals $-I/c$ directly; the force law (A2) then gives $p=I/c$ and $F/A=(I/c)e_3$. In (ii), the standing-wave fields are computed explicitly and averaged with [[lem-em-period-average]]; the cross terms cancel identically in $\\sigma_{33}$ because $|E|^2$ and $(c^2)$-weighted $|B|^2$ have opposite cross terms for the two counterpropagating waves, leaving $-\\varepsilon_0E_0^2=-2I/c$ at $0^-$, whence $p=2I/c$ and $F/A=(2I/c)e_3$ by (A2). The real-vector trig identities and the substitution $\\cos A\\pm\\cos B$, $\\sin A\\pm\\sin B$ are elementary; the averaged squares use $\\langle\\cos^2\\rangle=\\langle\\sin^2\\rangle=1/2$ from [[lem-em-period-average]]. The direction $\\hat k$ is $e_3$, the propagation direction of the incident beam. No material law, absorbed or reflected amplitude, or observed force is derived.",
        "physical_scope": "Adopted idealisations of a plane beam at normal incidence on an infinite plane ideal absorber or ideal reflector, with the pillbox force law as an explicitly stated model assumption; the conclusion is the conditional pressure $I/c$ or $2I/c$ along the propagation direction. Excluded: oblique incidence, finite beams and pulses, real material response, radiation reaction, measurements, and any empirical claim; $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is retained as an assumed premise.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 author-hosted revision, 236 pp.)",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
              "locator": "§4.4 pp. 92-94 (the Poynting flux and its period average for the incident wave, $\\bar S=c\\bar u\\hat k$)"
            },
            {
              "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall, 1999, 596-page retained scan)",
              "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
              "locator": "§9.2.3 printed p. 382 (eq. (9.64): radiation pressure on a perfect absorber is $I/c$, the incident momentum flux (energy flux divided by $c$), twice as great on a perfect reflector; the qualitative $qv\\times B$ mechanism)"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "ex-em-two-beam-fringes",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Ideal deterministic two-wave interference and its fringe geometry",
        "reference": "worked-example-verifications.md#X6",
        "design_state": "complete-exact-conditional-research-argument",
        "dependency_level": 21,
        "deps": [
          "pthm-em-classical-interference",
          "pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell",
          "def-em-plane-wave-definition",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "def-cross-product-in-r3",
          "def-em-si-conventions"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "pthm-em-classical-interference": "physical-result",
          "pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell": "physical-result",
          "def-em-plane-wave-definition": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-em-si-conventions": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $K>0$, $\\omega:=cK$, let $\\alpha\\in(0,\\pi/2)$ (half the angle between the\nbeams) and $\\varphi\\in\\mathbb R$, and take the two plane waves of\n[[def-em-plane-wave-definition]] with\n$$k_1=K(\\sin\\alpha\\,e_1+\\cos\\alpha\\,e_3),\\qquad k_2=K(-\\sin\\alpha\\,e_1+\\cos\\alpha\\,e_3),$$\n$$e_1^{\\mathrm{amp}}=E_0e_2,\\qquad e_2^{\\mathrm{amp}}=E_0e^{i\\varphi}e_2,$$\nwhere $E_0>0$ carries $\\mathrm V\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and both amplitudes are\npolarized along $e_2$, perpendicular to both wavevectors; with\n$b_j=k_j\\times e_j^{\\mathrm{amp}}/\\omega$ and $|k_j|=\\omega/c$ both pairs\nsatisfy the amplitude equations of [[pthm-em-plane-wave-maxwell]], so the\nsuperposition is a source-free smooth Maxwell solution\n([[pthm-em-classical-interference]]). Write\n$\\delta(x):=(k_2-k_1)\\cdot x=-2K\\sin\\alpha\\,x_1$. Then:\n\n(i) [Averaged energy density]\n$\\langle u\\rangle(x)=\\varepsilon_0E_0^2\\bigl[1+\\cos^2\\alpha\\,\\cos(2K\\sin\\alpha\\,x_1-\\varphi)\\bigr]$\nfor every $x$, by clause (b) of [[pthm-em-classical-interference]] with\n$e_1^{\\mathrm{amp}}\\cdot\\overline{e_2^{\\mathrm{amp}}}=E_0^2e^{-i\\varphi}$ and\n$(\\hat k_1\\times e_1^{\\mathrm{amp}})\\cdot(\\hat k_2\\times\\overline{e_2^{\\mathrm{amp}}})=E_0^2e^{-i\\varphi}\\cos2\\alpha$.\n\n(ii) [Fringes and visibility] At fixed $x_2,x_3$ the averaged energy density is\nperiodic in $x_1$ with period $\\pi/(K\\sin\\alpha)=\\lambda/(2\\sin\\alpha)$, where\n$\\lambda=2\\pi/K$; its maximum and minimum are\n$\\varepsilon_0E_0^2(1\\pm\\cos^2\\alpha)$, so the visibility\n$V:=\\frac{\\langle u\\rangle_{\\max}-\\langle u\\rangle_{\\min}}{\\langle u\\rangle_{\\max}+\\langle u\\rangle_{\\min}}$\nequals $\\cos^2\\alpha=\\frac12(1+\\hat k_1\\cdot\\hat k_2)$. In particular\n$V\\to1$ as $\\alpha\\downarrow0$ through crossing beams, and $V\\to0$ as\n$\\alpha\\to\\pi/2$. At $\\alpha=0$ with fixed relative phase the profile is\nspatially constant: its spatial visibility is $0$ when its value is positive,\nand the displayed ratio is undefined when the field vanishes.\n\n(iii) [The promised copropagating amplitude law] For $k_1=k_2=k$ with $|k|=K$ and $k\\cdot e_2=0$, retain the common linear\npolarization $e_2$ and amplitudes $E_0e_2$, $E_0e^{i\\Delta}e_2$ (so that the\nsum is the single plane wave with vector amplitude $E_0(1+e^{i\\Delta})e_2$),\n$\\langle u\\rangle=\\varepsilon_0E_0^2(1+\\cos\\Delta)=2\\varepsilon_0E_0^2\\cos^2(\\Delta/2)\\propto|1+e^{i\\Delta}|^2$,\nthe form used in the double-slit discussion of the content model. Scanning\n$\\Delta$ gives phase-scan visibility $1$, distinct from spatial visibility at\nfixed phase.\n\n(iv) [Statistical qualifications] This is a deterministic prediction of the\nclassical wave model for the specified coherent two-wave preparation, at every\npoint of the ideal plane-wave field; a *measured* fringe pattern or detector\nhistogram would additionally require a declared measurement model (detector\nresponse, finite bin width, background, integration time, sampling) and a\nstatistical analysis. The example reports no observation, no finite-sample\nclaim and no visibility estimate from data; in particular classical\nelectromagnetic waves interfere, so the prediction alone does not discriminate\nbetween classical and quantum descriptions of light. All quantities are\nidealized field quantities of the framework.",
        "strategy": "Specialise clause (b) of [[pthm-em-classical-interference]] to the stated amplitudes: $|e_j^{\\mathrm{amp}}|^2=E_0^2$, $e_1^{\\mathrm{amp}}\\cdot\\overline{e_2^{\\mathrm{amp}}}=E_0^2e^{-i\\varphi}$, and $\\hat k_1\\times e_2=\\sin\\alpha\\,e_3-\\cos\\alpha\\,e_1$, $\\hat k_2\\times e_2=-\\sin\\alpha\\,e_3-\\cos\\alpha\\,e_1$ give $(\\hat k_1\\times e_2)\\cdot(\\hat k_2\\times e_2)=\\cos2\\alpha$ by the coordinate cross product [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]; the formula (i) follows with $W=2E_0^2\\cos^2\\alpha\\,e^{-i(\\delta+\\varphi)}$. Part (ii) reads off the period, maxima, minima and visibility from (i). Part (iii) is clause (c) of [[pthm-em-classical-interference]] with $|a_1e_1+a_2e_2|^2=2E_0^2(1+\\cos\\Delta)$. Part (iv) is a scope statement: the field-theoretic identities are exact for the specified preparation, and no statistical model or dataset is introduced. No additional supplier is used.",
        "physical_scope": "A deterministic interference computation for two explicit ideal coherent plane waves of equal frequency and amplitude in a named inertial frame with SI units: averaged energy density, fringe period, visibility and the copropagating amplitude law. Excluded: real sources and detectors, finite coherence and bandwidth, sampling and noise models, finite-sample statistics, and any empirical claim; no discrimination between classical and quantum descriptions is asserted.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Electromagnetism, University of Cambridge Part II Mathematical Tripos lecture notes (2026 author-hosted revision, 236 pp.)",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/electromagnetism/electro.pdf",
              "locator": "§4.3.1 p. 86 (the principle of superposition: sums of monochromatic solutions are solutions and waves travelling in different directions pass through one another)"
            },
            {
              "title": "David J. Griffiths, Introduction to Electrodynamics, 3rd edition (Prentice Hall, 1999, 596-page retained scan)",
              "url": "https://www.hlevkin.com/hlevkin/90MathPhysBioBooks/Physics/Physics/Electrodynamics/David%20J.%20Griffiths%20-%20Introduction%20to%20Electrodynamics-Prentice%20Hall%20(1999).pdf",
              "locator": "§9.2.2 printed pp. 376-379 (linearity of the vacuum wave/Maxwell equations; the monochromatic amplitude representation on which the two-wave sum is written)"
            }
